/* book exam traps: A loss curve that jumps up and down, or turns into NaN, is divergence from a too-large η. It is not a local minimum. | One epoch is one step in batch gradient descent, not many. Students who assume otherwise get the training time wrong by a factor of N. | Forgetting the 1/N. Summing instead of averaging scales the gradient by N, which is the same as multiplying η by N. | Sign slip: θ ← θ + η∇L climbs the surface. Check by asking whether the loss went down. | η multiplies the gradient, so its size is tied to the gradient's scale. Changing the scale of the inputs changes the gradient and hence the effective step, so η must be re-tuned. */
