/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Full-batch gradient descent uses the average gradient over all N samples.
   - Mini-batch gradient descent uses a random set Bt of B sample indices.
   - θt is the parameter vector after t updates; η is the learning rate, a positive step size; Bt is the set of indices in batch t (size B); ∇ℓi(θt) is the gradient of sample i's loss at θt; B is the batch size.
   - Plain SGD is B = 1; some syllabi call it on-line learning.
   - The figure uses N = 12 and B = 4, so one epoch has three batches.
   - Draw the batch uniformly at random.
   - gB is the mini-batch gradient; σ2 is the variance of the single-sample gradient; B is the number of samples in the batch.
   - Doubling B halves the variance and divides the standard deviation by √2.
   - Linear scaling heuristic: if you multiply B by k, try multiplying η by k.
   - Shuffle the order every epoch.
*/
