/* book exam traps: Calling an epoch an iteration. An epoch has N/B iterations when batches are full; only full-batch descent has one update per epoch. | Comparing SGD steps with gradient descent epochs. With N = 1000 and B = 100, 20 SGD epochs are 200 updates, not 20. | Forgetting to shuffle. Fixed order makes batches correlated and can teach the model the order of classes rather than the features. | Using a tiny batch and blaming the learning rate. A very small B gives a high-variance gradient estimate; the direction itself is unreliable. | Treating linear scaling as a law. Multiplying B by k and η by k is only a starting guess; large steps can diverge. */
